The measured score on all 120 cases is 93%.
Here is what smaller eval sets would have told you, 2,000 resamples each.

   cases      95% of runs report   width
       5         60% to 100%         40%  ------------------------
      10         80% to 100%         20%  ------------
      20         80% to 100%         20%  ------------
      40         85% to 100%         15%  ---------
      80         88% to 99%          11%  -------
     120         88% to 98%           9%  ------
     300         90% to 96%           6%  ---
    1000         92% to 95%           3%  --

A 20-case eval set reports a number that is 20% wide. That is not a
measurement, it is a mood. Two systems five points apart are indistinguishable
to it, and five points is the size of most real improvements.

At 120 cases the interval is 9% wide, which is enough to tell a
ten-point change from noise and not enough to tell a three-point one. That is
the honest position of this chapter's eval set, and it is why §21.6 talks
about growing it rather than finishing it.

Note what the last two rows cost: nothing to compute here, and a thousand model
runs to actually have. Before paying for them, be clear about what size of
difference you need to detect — that number, not a round one, decides how many
cases you need.
